.TH PROC 3
.SH NAME
proc \- running processes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind #p /proc
.nf

.BI /proc/ n /status
.BI /proc/ n /ns
.BI /proc/ n /note
.BI /proc/ n /ctl
.BI /proc/ n /args
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I proc
device serves a directory of pids, one per live process, and each pid
directory holds the five files above.
The listing of
.B /proc
is the live pids, and a process that ends between two reads is simply not
there on the next one.
.PP
.B Status
is one line: the process's name, its pid, its parent's pid, its state, its
note group, and whether it is
.B detached
or
.BR held ,
and its working directory, each field followed by a space.
.IR Ps (1)
reads it.
.PP
.B Ns
is the process's name space, as
.I bind
and
.I mount
commands a script could run to build the same one.
The last line names the process's working directory.
.IR Ns (1)
reads it.
.PP
Writing to
.B note
posts a note to the process (see
.IR notify (2)):
a program that registered a handler is given the text and may carry on, and
one that did not ends with it as its status.
Reading
.B note
answers the oldest note posted and not yet delivered.
.PP
Writing to
.B ctl
controls the process:
.TP 8
.B kill
end the process at its next crossing into the kernel
.TP
.B stop
suspend the process there
.TP
.B start
resume a stopped process
.PD
.PP
.IR Kill (1)
writes
.B kill
here.
An unrecognised command fails with
.BR EINVAL .
.PP
.B Args
holds the arguments the process was given by the
.I exec
that started it (see
.IR exec (2)).
A process that has never called
.I exec
has an empty
.BR args .
Writing to
.B args
replaces its contents.
.B Status
and
.B ns
are read-only.
Writing to either fails with
.BR EPERM .
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/procfs
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (3),
.IR ps (1),
.IR ns (1),
.IR kill (1),
.IR notify (2),
.IR exec (2),
.IR fork (2),
.IR wait (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Every operation against a pid that has ended, or never existed, fails with
.BR ESRCH .
An unrecognised
.B ctl
command fails with
.BR EINVAL .
A write to
.B status
or
.B ns
fails with
.BR EPERM .
